{
  "image_name": "2c01f1d4ac565f516a010a0da8a3523a.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how *H. pylori* infection is associated with different pathological trajectories in the stomach, comparing the antrum and corpus. It uses stylized gastric glands to show how a healthy gland changes in cell composition and architecture after infection.\n\nIn the antrum, infection appears to drive hyperplastic gastritis, with expansion and rearrangement of the surface and glandular epithelium. In the corpus, the outcome is more diverse and severe: infection is linked to SPEM, then incomplete intestinal metaplasia and dysplasia, or alternatively to complete intestinal metaplasia. The visual emphasis is on progressive replacement of normal gastric cell types by abnormal or intestinal-like cells.\n\nThe healthy glands contain region-specific gastric lineages such as parietal, chief, mucous, enteroendocrine, and stem cells arranged along the gland axis. As disease develops, these normal populations diminish and are replaced by metaplastic and dysplastic cell states, including SPEM-like cells and goblet-cell–like intestinal cells. Colored dots around the glands suggest changing signaling environments involving factors such as EGF, BMP, WNT ligands, and RSPO3, with stromal cells also contributing. Overall, the figure depicts infection-driven remodeling of gastric glands toward metaplasia and neoplasia.",
